WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.826
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.340
ARIE MEIR: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.880
My name is Arie Meir.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.850
I'm a product manager in
Google Cloud health care.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.490
My team is focusing on
medical imaging solutions.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.150
So it's my privilege
and pleasure today

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:24.440
to host Dr. Kitamura, visiting
us from Hiroshima, Japan.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.390
He and his team are
here to tell you

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.700
a little bit about an
interesting company called

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.620
MNES and their work.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.550
So I actually learned
about MNES's work

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.520
from a friend of mine,
and one of the things

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.470
I found especially
interesting is

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.890
that they were able to build
a full tailored radiology

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.300
solution, cloud native--

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.210
so they started from the cloud--

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.060
and they did it
without ever speaking

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.950
to anyone technical in Google.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.220
So they're using AI, they're
using a full radiologist stack.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.722
They built their own viewer, and
they have full-time developers,

00:00:58.722 --> 00:01:00.680
they have full-time
radiologists on their team.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.030
They will tell you more--

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.690
but without ever
speaking to Google.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.800
So that's one of the examples
of a self-service customer.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.630
So I would like to start
by showing you a quick clip

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.209
to give you a
high-level overview,

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:14.750
and then we're going
to be discussing

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.255
different aspects of this work.

00:01:17.255 --> 00:01:20.720
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:03:12.016 --> 00:03:13.880
[END PLAYBACK]

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:14.380
Thank you.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.640
So before we move on
to the presentation,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.900
I would like to suggest you
look for the movie stars

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.210
among-- because there
are some people near you.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.640
But Dr. Kitamura,
welcome to Cloud Next.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.440
It's a pleasure having you here.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.815
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Thank you, too.

00:03:27.815 --> 00:03:28.790
It's great to be here.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.520
ARIE MEIR: So tell us a
little bit about MNES.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.220
What does your company do?

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.770
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: After
graduating from university,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:41.140
my first job was a radiologist
at a big hospital in Hiroshima.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.320
I saw money in [INAUDIBLE]
in clinical practice,

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.060
which I wanted to address.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.660
MNES is a teleradiologist
company based in Hiroshima,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.674
providing radiology scientist
to hospital [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:56.674 --> 00:03:58.090
ARIE MEIR: So what
was the problem

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.630
you were trying to address?

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.160
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
One day a patient

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.110
was brought to the hospital.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:10.420
She was 37-year-old with
advanced pancreatic cancer.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.660
I checked her previous
results of medical checkup

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.010
from six months ago.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:23.830
I examined the old CT image,
and yes, the cancer was there,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:24.940
and it was missed.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.700
ARIE MEIR: So how
does this happen?

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.210
How does one miss a cancer?

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.360
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: The
patient had her CT scan

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.730
at this small village
with limited access

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.100
to a radiologist.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:48.260
It was a misdiagnosis that
could have been prevented.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:53.030
This incident broke my
heart, and opened my eyes.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:59.870
This is why I started my company
MNES to focus on teleradiology

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:04.110
and bring expertise
where it's most needed.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:08.360
ARIE MEIR: So in your mind, how
does teleradiology help here?

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.330
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: Before I
explain how teleradiology

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:15.550
can help you, let me start
with you three dimensions

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.240
of the challenges.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.400
The first dimension
is the ratio of number

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.090
of a radiologists to the
number of images created.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.680
The second dimension is
geographical ignorance

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.550
of Japan as an island nation.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:40.160
And finally the third dimension
is the social uniqueness

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.992
of health care system.

00:05:41.992 --> 00:05:42.950
ARIE MEIR: Interesting.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.840
So what do you mean
when you say, the ratio?

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.730
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: Here is some
data on radiology [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.520
in Japan and the US.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.210
If you look at the number of CT
and MRI units in each country,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:02.660
you'll see that US and
Japan are pretty close.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.450
But if you look at the
number of radiologists,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.480
you will see that
USA has 10 times

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.640
the number of radiologists
compared to Japan.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.570
When I recently chatted
with an American colleague,

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:19.540
he was shocked that
a non-specialist

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:25.340
like an internist can make
a radiology interpretation.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:29.170
In the US, once a
mis-diagnosis happens,

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:33.460
it often leads to a
malpractice lawsuit.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.700
In Japan the situation
is different,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.252
and it is far from ideal.

00:06:40.252 --> 00:06:42.460
ARIE MEIR: So I understand
how this difference drives

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.990
you to make it more effective.

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:45.110
What about geography?

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.940
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: Because of the
geographical regions in Japan,

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:55.830
we also have a very uneven
distribution of doctors.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:01.330
Most of them are concentrated
in large urban areas.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:07.260
This makes rural areas very
starved for specialist access.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.150
My hometown is a
perfect example.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.980
Hiroshima prefecture
is surrounded

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.070
by islands and
mountainous areas,

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:20.350
with limited access
to specialists.

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:24.510
Compare it to the
map of California,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.900
and you can see how
teleradiology or telemedicine

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.162
become very relevant.

00:07:30.162 --> 00:07:30.870
ARIE MEIR: I see.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.880
So lots of islands,
difficult to access,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.461
all the experts are
centralized in big cities.

00:07:35.461 --> 00:07:37.710
What about-- you mentioned
there is a third dimension,

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:38.800
so social aspect.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.064
What do you mean by that?

00:07:40.064 --> 00:07:40.980
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: Yes.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.370
In the Japanese society
a female radiologist

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:49.290
often has to give up work to
support their young family.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:54.120
This contributes even more
to scarcity of radiologists.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.310
For these three reasons,
I believe teleradiology

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:00.792
is extremely relevant in Japan.

00:08:00.792 --> 00:08:01.500
ARIE MEIR: I see.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.500
So it makes sense because I
guess your platform enables

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.660
them to work part-time.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.940
Tell us how you
approach teleradiology.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.260
Are you a service company?

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:12.540
Are you a product company?

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.240
What exactly are
you focusing on?

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.330
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: MNES provides
a response in two ways.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.140
On the one hand, MNES
is a product company.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.260
Our product mainly is LOOKREC.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.440
On the other hand, MNES
is a service company.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.790
These two complement each other.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.690
ARIE MEIR: So what
exactly is the product?

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.110
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: Our product
enables the radiologist

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:42.179
to provide reading services
remotely to clinics.

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:48.444
Here's a movie of how we LOOKREC
software to make diagnosis.

00:08:48.444 --> 00:08:51.930
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:09:20.814 --> 00:09:21.810
[PLAYBACK ENDS]

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.840
ARIE MEIR: We Have
some technical issues.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:24.850
Just a second.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.250
Teleradiology platforms
have their limits.

00:09:30.645 --> 00:09:33.717
[INAUDIBLE REMARK FROM STAFF
 MEMBER]

00:09:33.717 --> 00:09:34.300
ARIE MEIR: OK.

00:09:37.626 --> 00:09:39.000
Let me try to
refresh this maybe.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.330
OK, we're going to
live without it.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:01.070
So basically, there
are two parts.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:04.600
One part is the reporting
tool, the other part

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:05.950
is the viewer tool.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.830
So the radiologist essentially
inspects the images

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.320
in the viewer, and
simultaneously creates

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:12.340
the report.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.650
You can also select which
slices you want to view,

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.830
and you can manipulate
the images in order

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.270
to make the correct diagnosis.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.990
OK, so tell us a little bit
about the service component,

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.470
Kitamura-san.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:47.210
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: MNES has
two types of radiologist.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.910
We have 11 full-time
radiologists

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:53.390
providing reading services
using the LOOKREC platform

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:55.910
to our partner clinics.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.760
We also have
part-time radiologists

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.940
to address changes in demand.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.960
We think of our platform
as Uber for radiology

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.109
to meet the dynamic
market demand.

00:11:08.109 --> 00:11:09.650
ARIE MEIR: So I hope
you're not going

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:13.850
to start hiring Uber drivers
for your teleradiologists.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:18.080
But tell us about who pays for
the teleradiology services.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.850
Is it the patient pays, or
is it the insurance company?

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.150
How does that work in Japan?

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.330
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: We have
customers for both options.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:30.180
All Japanese people have
government health care plan

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:34.160
which covers for some
radiology services.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.230
Some of our partners, for
example, Ginza Clinic,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.810
offer direct-to-consumer
imaging checkup,

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:48.110
which includes a brain MRI, plus
interpretation three experts--

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:52.880
radiologist, cardiologist,
and neuroradiologist

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.127
for less than $200.

00:11:56.127 --> 00:11:56.960
ARIE MEIR: So just--

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.300
this was a shocking number
for me when I heard this.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:03.710
So basically Japanese are known
to be very interested in health

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:07.420
checkups, so there are private
companies, direct-to-consumer,

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:12.230
that offer a brain MRI scan
for $200, completely paid

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.560
by the customer, that offer
a checkup by a radiologist,

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.070
a neuroradiologist, and a
cardiologist, for aneurysms.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.300
So how does that make sense?

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.810
Because this number,
$200, is extremely low

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.730
compared to the US,
where a brain MRI

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:28.490
can cost north of $2000.

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:32.090
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: That's
a complicated question.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:35.210
Let me offer two data points.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:43.250
A typical salary for Japanese
radiologist is around $120,000.

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.599
Do you know what is a typical
salary for a US radiologist.

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:47.890
ARIE MEIR: I think it's higher.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.710
So just, I want to
reiterate number.

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:52.940
So I'm not a radiologist,
but the numbers

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.680
are like three or
four times higher.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:58.329
$120,000 for a
radiologist in Japan.

00:12:58.329 --> 00:12:59.870
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Another data point.

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:05.570
Malpractice insurance in
Japan costs $600 a year.

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:08.420
In the US, my understanding
is that this number

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:09.050
is much higher.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.550
ARIE MEIR: Does
anyone actually know

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.170
how much malpractice
for radiologists

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.485
cost in a representative state?

00:13:14.485 --> 00:13:17.549
AUDIENCE: So then 15 years
ago it was around $50,000.

00:13:17.549 --> 00:13:18.340
ARIE MEIR: $50,000.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:19.580
And it depends on state.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:21.680
It varies if you--
depends where you live,

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.170
between $20,000 and $200,000.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.060
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: So
I'm not sure about that.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:28.610
The other fact is that
this difference really

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.885
contributes to the cost.

00:13:30.885 --> 00:13:32.510
ARIE MEIR: Yeah, so
I understand if you

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:35.600
have your own radiologist, or
if you don't have a radiologist

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:39.860
but you have imaging equipment,
you can benefit from LOOKREC.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.289
How about clinics that don't
even have imaging devices?

00:13:44.289 --> 00:13:45.830
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Very good question.

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:49.085
For this case, we all
mobile CT service.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.840
Mobile CT's are a dedicated
truck loaded with a CT scanner.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:03.390
It helps us serve rural
areas without a CT scanner.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:06.600
We drive this mobile
CT to where it's needed

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.600
and get people scanned.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:12.720
We also use it for
employee screening

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:17.160
since Japanese law requires
employers to offer this service

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.347
to their employees once a year.

00:14:20.347 --> 00:14:22.430
ARIE MEIR: So this was a
very interesting insight.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:25.560
So my team had the privilege
of visiting MNES in Japan

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.950
a couple of months ago, and
we actually visited this van.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:30.470
We were inside this van.

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:33.270
And Kitamura-san's
technician offered

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:34.890
us to run a presentation.

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:36.270
So none of us
wanted to volunteer

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:39.150
for ionizing
radiation, but we had

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.730
the little anecdote I remember.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.450
So we had the little
bento box lunch

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.570
that the technician
had that day,

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.900
and he said that his
wife made it for him.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.790
And he said, I don't
know what's in it.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:51.610
I have not seen it.

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.750
So he put it in the
CT and he scanned it,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:56.580
and we were able to
figure out the rice,

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:58.500
and the little tamagoyaki.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.750
It was like lots of
interesting insights.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.750
AUDIENCE: I'm sorry, by
law, they have to give--

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:07.170
ARIE MEIR: So employers in
Japan are required by law

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:09.960
to offer an annual checkup
that includes a lung cancer

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.000
screening.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.270
Very interesting difference.

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:15.260
AUDIENCE: They don't
worry about dose?

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.010
ARIE MEIR: That's an
interesting question.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.930
Yeah, I think there is
a different approach

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.410
to risk management in Japan.

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.930
It was insightful
for me as well.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.030
I think that they also
try to striate patients.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:27.820
So they-- obviously
you kind of have

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.160
to hit the right
demographics, and then it

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.050
becomes more relevant.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:36.580
So Kitamura-san, what is your
vision for MNES in the future?

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.080
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
So far I have been

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:42.020
talking about teleradiology
model as it is now.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:45.040
But this is only a first step.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:48.180
In the future I
envision three steps.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:54.100
First, teleradiology;
second, image-based AI,

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:58.560
algorithms that primarily look
at the images for prediction

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:05.650
AI; third, holistic AI,
algorithms that look at other

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:06.880
data--

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:11.750
genomics, demographics,
nutrition --for prediction.

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:12.730
ARIE MEIR: I see.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:16.330
So I understand what image-based
AI is, so you primarily

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:17.050
look at images.

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:19.760
Can you tell us more
specifics about it?

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.900
How are you using AI?

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.620
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Image diagnosis in AI

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:29.300
is algorithms that use images
as primary data source.

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:31.620
We have started working
with different types

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.280
of AI applications.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:39.670
Let me give you three examples
we are already using today.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:43.910
AI that can detect
brain aneurysm,

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:47.580
which is abnormal blood vessel.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:52.960
Breast cancer is already used
in daily clinical practice.

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:58.300
CT lung nodule AI detector
for cancer diagnostics.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:03.460
Interesting to note that the AI
models have different quality.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:07.690
For example, AI for
brain aneurysm detection

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:11.810
has 85% sensitivity,
which makes it

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:17.890
a useful tool to double-check
human radiologist.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:20.680
Let's play a movie to
demonstrate how we are

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.694
using AI in LOOKREC product.

00:17:22.694 --> 00:17:24.069
ARIE MEIR: So this
was actually--

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:26.290
before we play the movie,
I want to point out

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:30.220
that this was a fascinating
discovery because MNES is using

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.895
AI algorithms already embedded
in their clinical workflow,

00:17:33.895 --> 00:17:35.770
and these algorithms
are still in the process

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.690
of clinical validation.

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:40.480
But the specific algorithm,
the brain aneurysm Kitamura-san

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:42.280
mentioned-- the brain--

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:46.420
the breast cancer
detection for MRI images,

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.352
and the CT lung nodule detector.

00:17:48.352 --> 00:17:49.810
It was very
interesting, especially

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:51.449
to see the feedback
collection piece,

00:17:51.449 --> 00:17:52.740
which you will see in a second.

00:17:57.504 --> 00:17:58.920
I hope there is
sound on this one.

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:03.820
No sound.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:14.350
OK, so I'm going to make
a voiceover for this.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.140
So essentially
what you see here,

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.480
the pictures are being
sent to the cloud.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.756
The AI detects what is
the right algorithm.

00:18:20.756 --> 00:18:22.380
In this case, this
is a brain aneurysm,

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.780
and it detects that
this is a brain MRI,

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.180
so you have to apply
[INAUDIBLE] and then

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:30.240
the AI operates by detecting
all the places where

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.780
it suspects aneurysm to appear.

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.150
All the results of the AI
are here on the right side,

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.260
and what the screen allows
the radiologist to do

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:40.930
is to provide feedback.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:43.830
So for each one of these
findings, the radiologist has

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:48.470
to say, true positive, which
means that AI was right,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:51.380
or it marks as false
positive, which

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.800
means that the AI was wrong.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.220
Once the feedback has collected,
you can press Send Feedback,

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.760
and that is being sent to the
developer of the algorithm.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.430
Obviously sometimes
the AI misses things.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.300
This is the false negatives.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.200
So this is an opportunity
for the doctor to provide--

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:13.290
to point and click where
the AI missed the diagnosis.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.370
So this information is being
used by the application

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:19.984
developer in order to improve
the algorithm continuously.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:31.860
So this is how the
user invokes the AI,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.490
and this is the
feedback screen again.

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:40.850
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: I use
this AI system every day.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:51.120
Image diagnosis with
AI will be the standard

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:53.380
in the near future.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:58.110
But we radiologists should
not rely too much on AI.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.770
If you trust AI completely
and don't make diagnosis

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:04.950
on your own, your
diagnostic ability

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:06.670
will decline in no time.

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.300
ARIE MEIR:
[INAUDIBLE] thank you.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:11.220
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: We should
keep on upgrading our ability,

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:15.480
and send the diagnostic
results to AI.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.150
That would improve
AI's accuracy,

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.889
therefore it would be
a benefit for patients.

00:20:20.889 --> 00:20:22.680
ARIE MEIR: So you know
the loaded question,

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:23.860
the elephant in the room.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:27.360
Are there any
radiologists in the room?

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:29.290
OK, maybe no elephant here.

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:34.440
But I can tell you, when we gave
a similar talk in RSNA, which

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:36.870
is the Radiology Society
of North America, which

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.650
is 30,000 radiologists getting
in the middle of the winter,

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:41.790
in Chicago.

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:44.160
The question is like,
so what's going on here?

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.440
Is AI going to
replace radiologists?

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.620
So Kitamura-san, what
is your take on this?

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:53.170
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: This is
indeed a loaded question.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.690
I know that there
is a healthy debate

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:58.860
in my professional
radiology community.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:02.860
Here is what I think will
happen in the near future.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:06.750
First, we will see
that over time, AI

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:10.680
will keep bringing more and
more value as a decision support

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:16.320
tool to help human radiologists
make more precise decisions.

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:20.400
Second, we know that human
radiologists are valued

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:26.430
for their experience, so we will
help human radiologist focus

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:28.800
on the truly difficult cases.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:29.700
ARIE MEIR: Hm.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.460
So one additional data
point I would like to add

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:35.100
is that the meme you
hear in this space

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:38.720
is that AI is not going
to replace radiologists,

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:42.030
but the radiologists
that are using AI

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.044
will replace radiologists
that are not using AI.

00:21:45.044 --> 00:21:46.460
Because it is like
saying, OK, I'm

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:48.300
not going to use the internet.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:50.070
But if this is a
tool that allows

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.050
you to be more productive
and provide better

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:54.930
care for your patients,
you are not competitive

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.760
if you're not going to use it.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:00.240
So another thing is to think
about AI in this context,

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.940
it's not one black
box that solves

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.100
all the problems
of the world, just

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:09.300
like an auto-pilot in a
plane is not one instrument.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.950
It is a collection--
if you ever looked

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.240
into a cockpit of a
commercial airline,

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:15.630
you know that it's a
collection of instruments.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:18.330
Each one is very dedicated,
very focused, and solves

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.080
a very particular problem.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.930
Now an auto-pilot
is a collection,

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:25.110
and they all work in
tandem with each other,

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:28.330
and they help the pilot
make better decisions.

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:31.600
The pilot is still the one
who makes the final decisions,

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:35.400
especially in the complicated
cases like taking off, landing,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.240
or during turbulence.

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:42.610
So the same thing it is my view
that will happen in this space.

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:44.500
We will see more
and more tools--

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.390
like the ones that
Kitamura-san mentioned

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:49.120
--developed and being
helpful to radiologists

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.470
to make them more productive.

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:54.010
So I also think that there
are a couple of questions

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:56.440
that the whole space is trying
to figure out specifically

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:59.050
around regulatory approval.

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.620
How do you think about
FDA approval of AI model

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:04.730
that by definition is not fixed.

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:06.260
You know, medical device--

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:06.760
fixed.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:09.490
You sign off the sheets,
you manufacture it,

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:10.730
and you're done.

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:12.460
If you want to make
a new device you

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.730
need to apply for
regulatory approval again.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:18.040
AI, by definition, is
something evolving.

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:21.010
So FDA right now
is also evolving

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:24.700
in how it thinks about
regulatory approval for AI

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.090
applications.

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:28.570
It also-- will actually
work with the FDA

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:31.480
closely in their
program where they

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:34.150
try to adjust their approach.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.370
Specifically, until
now, the FDA has

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:38.920
been focusing on providing
regulatory approval

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:40.210
to products.

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:42.850
Now they're more
experimenting with the idea

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:46.500
that they can provide regulatory
approval to a process.

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:48.340
So if you are compliant
with a process,

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:50.280
and they approve
that process, it

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:54.280
allows you to have more freedom
to operate an experiment.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:55.820
Very exciting times.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.130
So Kitamura-san, thank you.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:23:58.870
These were great
examples, I think,

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:00.880
and the feedback
collection piece I think

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:02.620
is very interesting.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:05.060
Can you talk a little
bit about holistic AI,

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:06.970
and your vision for that?

00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:08.500
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Holistic AI means

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:11.230
using more than imaging data.

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:15.550
We want to help patients
collect different data types--

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:20.770
medical record data, genomics
data, nutrition data,

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:26.340
medication data, exercise
data, sleep data.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:32.030
Think of LOOKREC as Google
Drive for medical records.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:35.450
Once this data is in
the cloud, MNES AI

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:41.770
can analyze it and make
useful recommendations.

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:45.980
We have already started a
centralized management system,

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:49.030
including medical
interviews, medical cure,

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:54.070
and pathological imaging, in
addition to radiologic imaging.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:59.610
To integrate all the data,
we use a Google account.

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:02.010
Here in this room, some
of you may have already

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:06.530
started working on
innovative AI applications.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.810
We are very interested to
collaborate with partners

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:12.720
to accelerate our mission.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.891
Please come talk to us.

00:25:14.891 --> 00:25:16.640
ARIE MEIR: So Kitamura-san,
until now, you

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.770
have been focusing on Japan.

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:20.340
Are you only working in Japan?

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:22.920
Are you thinking of going
outside of the country?

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:24.336
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Thank you, Arie.

00:25:24.336 --> 00:25:26.430
Your question has good timing.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:30.120
We are very interested
to go global.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:32.460
Let me give you an example.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.670
This month, we launched
a collaborative project

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:40.260
between Japan and Mongolia to
help doctors in that country

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:44.960
improve their radiologic
diagnostic quality.

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:48.590
The way it works is through
the network system of MNES

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.640
that connect the two countries.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:55.090
When diagnostic radiologists
or pathologists in Mongolia

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:58.300
face a difficult
case, they can consult

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:02.010
with experts based in Japan.

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:05.880
It's similar to having senior
doctors advising junior doctors

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.750
in a teaching setting.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:12.750
This helps patients,
and also helps improve

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.600
skills for Mongolian doctors.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:16.970
ARIE MEIR: Very interesting.

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:19.760
So Kitamura-san, you
are now in Cloud Next.

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:22.040
Tell us a little about
your cloud journey.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:25.892
How did you end up
choosing-- why Google?

00:26:25.892 --> 00:26:27.350
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
Many of our staff

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:31.450
already use Google services,
including Gmail, Calendar,

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.770
and Drive.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:35.840
We wanted to build
the teleradiology

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:38.945
as a service as
simple as logging

00:26:38.945 --> 00:26:42.090
in to your Gmail account.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:49.950
Also Google Drive is a place
where people store their files.

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:52.560
We want to build a place
where people can store

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:57.180
their medical images and data.

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:00.860
We also wanted partner
of measurable size

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:05.540
that would enable us to develop
telemedicine services that

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:10.350
would be reliable and scalable.

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:17.310
We also have global goals, and
we wanted a global partner.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:21.310
Most importantly we
saw Google as a partner

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:26.060
with a vision that
is aligned to ours,

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:32.150
democratized access to
high-quality care, universally.

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:35.121
We see our goal achieved
through telemedicine.

00:27:35.121 --> 00:27:37.370
ARIE MEIR: So it sounds like
you had quite a checklist

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.286
for your requirements.

00:27:38.286 --> 00:27:40.729
I'm happy that we were able
to meet your expectations,

00:27:40.729 --> 00:27:41.270
Kitamura-san.

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:42.620
Thank you.

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:46.170
I wanted to ask you about some
of the challenges around cloud.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:50.630
So in the US we're learning that
health space, financial space,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.380
and other regulated
industries like government,

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.400
they're slowly
warming up to the idea

00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:58.610
of using clouds for their
data storage and processing.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:00.080
How is this happening in Japan?

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:01.940
What kind of challenges
were you facing

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:04.280
when you were approaching
health care providers?

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:09.740
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: We have indeed
observed a gradual increase

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:13.740
in willingness to use cloud
solutions in the health care

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:17.550
space, and we think
it's very exciting.

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:21.070
We think it has to
do with two reasons.

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:25.250
First, large financial
institutions in Japan

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:29.280
are moving to the cloud, and
that sends a strong signal

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:33.100
to other industries
like health care.

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:38.650
Second, because Japan
produces so much imaging data,

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:42.490
the cost efficiency of
storing the data on-premise

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:47.190
becomes less and less
economical for hospitals.

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:49.740
They are looking for
effective solutions,

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:52.962
and here cloud can
provide real value.

00:28:52.962 --> 00:28:53.920
ARIE MEIR: Interesting.

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:55.294
So when you're
deploying LOOKREC,

00:28:55.294 --> 00:28:58.110
is it like a pure cloud,
or a hybrid approach?

00:28:58.110 --> 00:28:59.760
How does that work?

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:01.140
NAOYUKI KITAMURA:
In the present,

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:03.420
because many of
our customers are

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.180
asking for additional
backup storage,

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:08.852
we offer a hybrid solution.

00:29:08.852 --> 00:29:13.440
We keep most of the data in
the data center in Tokyo,

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:18.250
and some of the data
on-premise in the hospital.

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:22.230
We also know that Google is
planning to open another data

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:24.900
center in Osaka.

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:30.240
This will enable us to move to a
pure cloud solution, which will

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.872
make our IT management easier.

00:29:33.872 --> 00:29:34.830
ARIE MEIR: Makes sense.

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:37.520
So as we are about
to wrap up, what

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:39.750
are you most excited about
the future, Kitamura-san?

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:42.960
Where do you see the
most opportunity here?

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:46.010
NAOYUKI KITAMURA: I'm
excited about three things.

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:52.540
First, I want to see MNES become
a truly global initiative,

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:57.730
bringing specialist expertise
where it's needed the most.

00:29:57.730 --> 00:30:02.980
Second, we want to advance the
role of AI in teleradiology.

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:06.310
We want to integrate
more AI algorithms

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:09.270
into our clinical work.

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:12.450
Third, we want to
find partners that

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:16.935
can use our platform to solve
problems like rare diseases.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:24.980
If any of these resonate with
you, please come talk to us.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:29.850
We are welcoming
potential collaboration.

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:33.090
We would to make
LOOKREC a tool that

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:36.080
can improve patients' lives.

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:40.670
This is our mission, and
nothing would make me happier

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:41.462
than that.

00:30:41.462 --> 00:30:42.920
ARIE MEIR: Thank
you, Kitamura-san.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:45.320
I think this is going
to-- to wrap up,

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:48.560
one of the examples of a
very fruitful partnership--

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:52.880
because LOOKREC brings a lot
of the products Google is

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:56.180
building, showcasing what
is possible to do with them,

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:59.210
and it also enables other
partners, the ones that

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:02.030
develop the AI
applications, to be tested,

